1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,080
This is Clark, and I want to take a moment to thank the hundreds of scouters all over the world who sponsor the podcast by being ScoutmasterCG.com backers and patrons.

2
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:17,700
You can join them by going to ScoutmasterCG.com and following the support or be a patron links at the top of the page.

3
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:20,180
And now it's the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:28,460
So we're driving to a camp out.

5
00:00:28,759 --> 00:00:30,460
One of the scouts in the car turns to me and says,

6
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:34,160
How is it that the deer know to cross at the deer crossing signs?

7
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,840
Endless entertainment, right?

8
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:39,840
Oh, well.

9
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,840
Hey, this is Scoutmaster podcast number 35.

10
00:00:50,180 --> 00:01:13,400
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

11
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,420
This is Clark Green.

12
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:21,140
In this edition, we're going to continue our discussion about youth troop leadership and the patrol system.

13
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:23,840
And I've got a Scoutmaster Minute built in there.

14
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:30,460
But I wanted to take a couple of minutes to talk to you about a book that I have found.

15
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:36,300
And I've actually found this book, oh, probably 10 or 12 years ago.

16
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:44,040
But I have found that somebody went to the trouble of making a PDF version of it and putting it online.

17
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:49,260
And I recommend it to you if you're a student of history or you have an interest in the history of scouting.

18
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:52,280
The title of the book is The Left Handshake.

19
00:01:52,920 --> 00:02:02,720
And the subtitle is The Boy Scout Movement During the War, 1939 to 1945 by Hillary St. George Saunders.

20
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:06,380
And this was originally published in 1949.

21
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:17,820
And The Left Handshake is a record of what happened to scouting during the Second World War, not only in Great Britain, but in occupied Germany.

22
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:20,380
I think most of the time, or occupied Europe.

23
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:33,519
I think most of the time we're pretty familiar with that part of our history of the Boy Scouts of America and the scrap drives and bond drives that scouts participated in over here.

24
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:41,960
And we were fortunate enough not to have been occupied or attacked on our shores to a great extent during the Second World War.

25
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:47,460
But what happened to scouts who were in countries who were overrun and occupied by the Nazis?

26
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,060
It's very, very interesting history.

27
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:53,800
And I would recommend it to you.

28
00:02:53,880 --> 00:03:01,700
I will put a link on the blog to where you can find a PDF copy of the book, The Left Handshake.

29
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:20,660
Now, there's a personal connection that I have to that particular history in that 22 years ago this summer, I was part of the, oh gosh, I think it was the 8th quadrennial jamboree of the Polish scout movement in exile.

30
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:27,500
Now, the Polish scouting movement was a very strong movement before the war.

31
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:36,019
And, of course, when the Nazis overran Poland, they basically did away with scouting as the Poles knew it.

32
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:54,940
And during that time period as Poles escaped from the occupation and spread out all over the world and actually subsequent to the war, you know, during the diaspora time where they ended up going all over the world, they also took their scouting movement with them.

33
00:03:54,940 --> 00:04:03,060
And that scouting movement, as of 22 years ago, was still alive and well in all these different countries.

34
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:05,680
And it was run just as it was run in Poland.

35
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:20,680
And it was a vehicle for the families who had dispersed from Poland to teach their children Polish and some of the Polish national traditions and to involve them in scouting at the same time.

36
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:27,080
And every four years, they would get together someplace in the world and have a jamboree.

37
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:33,800
And they chose our scout camp as the location for that jamboree in 1988.

38
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:41,919
About 2,000 adults and youth came to our camp and celebrated the Polish scouting movement in exile.

39
00:04:41,919 --> 00:04:53,000
And it was interesting because it was the first time that they had had these events since the war where a scout unit from Poland actually managed to get out to attend the event.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:04,700
If you remember, in 1988, Lekwilenza and Solidarity were just beginning to make an impact in the political situation in Poland, which was still a communist government.

41
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:12,820
And so it was very exciting that these folks got out and were able to join their fellow scouts from all over the world in 1988.

42
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:29,440
But I think one of the more particularly touching things, and it was very revelatory to me, was a reunion of those people who were present who had continued their scouting tradition in the concentration camps during the war.

43
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:30,659
Can you believe that?

44
00:05:30,659 --> 00:05:41,320
I mean, these were pretty small in number at the time because this was 40 years after the end of the Second World War.

45
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:57,000
But these were folks who had been in concentration camps during the war and who had continued to use the scouting program to help them survive and to help them stay encouraged and help them stay together, which was pretty remarkable.

46
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:14,680
I was also introduced to a man who was a scoutmaster during that time and who was in his late 80s at the time and who had taught his scouts to be saboteurs and taught them how to blow up trains and to generally interfere with the occupation forces.

47
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:24,260
So you'll find a lot of this reflected in this history by Hillary St. George Sanders, the one that's called the Left Handshake.

48
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:28,660
And I will have a link to that on the blog so that you can read it in PDF format.

49
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:34,920
Well, I thought that that might interest some of you, but we'll go ahead and move along because we've got a lot to do.

50
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:36,480
So let's get started, shall we?

51
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:40,940
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

52
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:54,200
Here we are with our second installment on building strong youth leadership and implementing the patrol system.

53
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:58,700
Now, the last time we talked a little, we tried to lay some basic ideas out.

54
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:07,760
And the basic idea that I left you with is that this is an attitude adjustment, a change of outlook on the part of adults.

55
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:11,240
We need to educate ourselves about the program.

56
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:14,120
My suggestion is that you look at it totally new.

57
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,800
Look at it with a new set of eyes.

58
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:25,240
One way to get the real basis of what happens in scouting is to go back and to read Baden Powell's little booklet called Aids to Scout Mastership.

59
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:30,160
It's a very dated booklet as far as language goes and some of the terminology.

60
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:40,800
But if you read it carefully, you will develop an appreciation for what the founder, Baden Powell, was doing when he developed the scouting program in the way that he did.

61
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,140
I will have a link to that on the blog.

62
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:54,340
Part of this process is going to be replacing working towards some idealized version of scouting with immersing your scouts in a process.

63
00:07:54,340 --> 00:07:55,979
That's what the program is.

64
00:07:56,099 --> 00:07:56,919
It's a process.

65
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:01,060
Scouting is founded not on a set of easily quantifiable goals.

66
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:04,679
We aren't building efficiently functioning organizational units.

67
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:06,900
We're helping young men grow up into good people.

68
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,200
And that's a process.

69
00:08:08,679 --> 00:08:14,679
We're giving them a field of play and endeavor that has a broad definition of success so they can all succeed.

70
00:08:15,239 --> 00:08:23,000
We're planting seeds of personal responsibility, character, and virtue that grow not through a series of planned events, but through a process.

71
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:30,920
If your picture of scouting right now is a rank and file of perfectly behaved, perfectly uniform, perfectly skilled scouts, I got news.

72
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:31,960
You're going to be disappointed.

73
00:08:32,659 --> 00:08:42,700
You have to accept that scouting is a process of giving boys the autonomy, responsibility, and authority to discover their own abilities and develop them at their own speed.

74
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:44,340
We can't make them grow.

75
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:48,260
We can only create and preserve the conditions that encourage growth.

76
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:51,980
That's the big basic underlying concept of the scout program.

77
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:56,420
Now, I've heard all of the excuses about why this won't work.

78
00:08:56,640 --> 00:09:00,580
That is that, you know, scouts won't take their leadership positions seriously.

79
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,740
They won't plan things very well.

80
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:11,080
And the whole thing will go spinning out of control if the adults aren't right there making plans and leading and making things happen.

81
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:13,860
We want things to go spinning out of control.

82
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:15,240
We want mistakes to be made.

83
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:16,660
We want things to be imperfect.

84
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:24,860
And that's hard to do if you have a vision of scouting that is aiming at some kind of perfect ideal.

85
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:28,319
Forget about the perfect ideal and think about the process.

86
00:09:28,719 --> 00:09:31,180
You have to remember who we're talking about here.

87
00:09:31,319 --> 00:09:33,060
We're talking about adolescent boys.

88
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:36,640
And they are one of the most unstable elements known to mankind.

89
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:37,920
They're capricious.

90
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:39,319
They're short-sighted.

91
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:40,460
They're inexperienced.

92
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:41,300
They're immature.

93
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:42,439
And they are naive.

94
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:54,920
Now, one sure way to keep them capricious, short-sighted, inexperienced, immature, and naive is to ask them to sit quietly by and let us more experienced, stable, and mature adults do all the leadership.

95
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:58,660
I mean, otherwise, how are they going to be able to earn merit badges and advance in rank?

96
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,599
How will they learn to do anything?

97
00:10:00,959 --> 00:10:02,319
How will they develop in character?

98
00:10:02,459 --> 00:10:03,180
How will they mature?

99
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:11,500
All these things will happen by them being a scout and doing the things that scouts do and going through the process that is the scouting program.

100
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:12,620
Why is this true?

101
00:10:12,719 --> 00:10:14,099
Because the program works.

102
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:22,160
So once we have a really good appreciation of the program, of the process involved in it, then we have to start changing things.

103
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:27,160
Now, these changes will often be sweeping fundamental changes.

104
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:31,339
Now, scouts are creatures of habit, but they're also excited and energized by change.

105
00:10:31,439 --> 00:10:32,860
They take on change very readily.

106
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:35,880
They don't often find it upsetting or off-putting.

107
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:40,000
Adults, on the other hand, usually pretty reluctant to change.

108
00:10:40,079 --> 00:10:40,939
We find it upsetting.

109
00:10:41,100 --> 00:10:42,160
We find it disquieting.

110
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:43,199
We find it strange.

111
00:10:43,780 --> 00:10:48,740
Much of the time, we're very resistant to change, almost to the point of irrational fear.

112
00:10:48,740 --> 00:10:52,420
And I said we because I include myself in the picture.

113
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:53,800
I'm not preaching to you.

114
00:10:54,420 --> 00:11:00,460
I'm trying to couch all of this in the most friendly and understanding way possible because I've been there.

115
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:03,120
In practical day-to-day terms, what does this look like?

116
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:05,960
Well, frankly, it looks disorganized and messy.

117
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:09,000
And that's okay because that's what we want it to look like.

118
00:11:09,140 --> 00:11:10,440
I'll arrive at our troop meeting.

119
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:11,460
I open the doors.

120
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:17,080
And shortly thereafter, the senior patrol leader and the patrol leaders and the other youth leaders will arrive.

121
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:24,260
Now, I might ask the senior patrol leader if he has everything ready to go for the evening or ask him to review the preparations with me.

122
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:31,360
And I try and stay out of his hair because usually he's preparing things and getting things ready.

123
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:36,340
And I'll tell you whatever he answers, I usually just say, good, it sounds like a plan.

124
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:37,920
What do you need from me?

125
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:40,140
His typical answer is nothing.

126
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:44,900
So, as the other adults arrive, we'll be in a room away from the main meeting room.

127
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,140
And we'll stay there.

128
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:47,000
We'll catch up.

129
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:51,800
We'll swap some stories for a while until one of the scouts alerts us to the opening ceremony.

130
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:53,900
Then we'll go into the main meeting room.

131
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:56,800
You know, there's the scout oath, the law, the Pledge of Allegiance.

132
00:11:56,939 --> 00:12:00,060
We listen to the senior patrol leader talk about what's going to happen at the meeting.

133
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:05,640
And we'll go back to our room and the scouts will go ahead and proceed with their meeting plans.

134
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:09,280
We adults, well, we usually have a few bits of business to discuss.

135
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:14,420
I may have a couple of scoutmaster conferences or other discussions with individual scouts.

136
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:20,760
And now, I don't require the adults just to sit in this room and not look at anything.

137
00:12:20,959 --> 00:12:24,439
They are free to silently observe what is going on with the scouts.

138
00:12:24,699 --> 00:12:26,439
And I emphasize silently.

139
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:34,500
Indeed, I will ask some of them to observe and be ready to offer observations later on in the meeting.

140
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:38,800
But we kind of have a rule about interfering in the middle of a meeting.

141
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:40,360
It's the boys' time.

142
00:12:40,720 --> 00:12:41,500
They're on the field.

143
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:42,520
They're playing the game.

144
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:44,460
We're watching from the sidelines.

145
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:49,400
And we're going to be majorly silent and be in the position of observers.

146
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:52,060
One of the scouts will tell us when it's time for a closing.

147
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:53,760
We'll go back to the meeting room.

148
00:12:53,939 --> 00:12:56,000
The senior patrol leader will make his announcements.

149
00:12:56,780 --> 00:12:59,520
And then he'll turn things over to me for my scoutmaster's minute.

150
00:12:59,660 --> 00:13:03,579
Sometimes this will be a deep thought or an announcement.

151
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:09,100
Or sometimes I just have a pretty lame joke, in the opinion of my scouts, at least.

152
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:11,900
We close with the scoutmaster's benediction.

153
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:16,840
And then the patrol leader's council will meet for 10 or 15 minutes after the meeting.

154
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:19,800
After this meeting, the senior patrol leader reviews the meeting they just had.

155
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:24,280
They talk about what went well, what didn't go well, and what they need to work on.

156
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:27,060
He'll also review the preparations for the next couple of weeks.

157
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:29,000
I might have something to say.

158
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:30,420
I might want to ask some questions.

159
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:34,480
And may ask if our assistant scoutmasters have anything to offer.

160
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:38,180
And the assistant scoutmasters might be there in the room for this meeting.

161
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:42,900
But they understand that during the patrol leader's council meetings, they can listen quietly.

162
00:13:43,420 --> 00:13:45,940
And if they have a question or a comment, they can raise their hand

163
00:13:45,940 --> 00:13:48,100
until they're recognized by the senior patrol leader.

164
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:52,740
The week directly after our monthly outing, our meeting night is a patrol leader's council

165
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:54,200
combined with a board of review.

166
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:57,360
Now, the patrol leader's council will meet to make plans.

167
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:59,780
They meet on their own, without any adults in the room.

168
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:02,440
Once they've made their plans, they let me know.

169
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:04,360
And myself and the other adults come in.

170
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:07,860
And they listen to this senior patrol leader review the plans with us.

171
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:11,700
We may ask questions or make comments on the plans if we need to.

172
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:14,340
But we're used to making our comments brief.

173
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:19,140
On occasion, after this review, the patrol leader's council might need to reconvene and

174
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:19,960
work a bit more.

175
00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:22,380
But often, that's the end of the evening.

176
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:26,540
When we go camping, the youth leadership meets a bit early on a Friday evening, typically.

177
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:28,140
They get ready for the scouts.

178
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:32,800
They have made their gear, transportation, and program plans ahead of time.

179
00:14:32,920 --> 00:14:34,140
Hopefully, they've done that.

180
00:14:34,760 --> 00:14:37,480
And at one point, the senior patrol leader will tell me they're ready to go.

181
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:38,980
We load up and we take off.

182
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:40,600
We get to the camping spot.

183
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:44,080
And the senior patrol leader and his patrol leaders look at the campground.

184
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:45,740
They decide how they're going to set it up.

185
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:46,520
And we get with it.

186
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:49,880
The adults in attendance are assigned an area to set up.

187
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:55,060
And it is far enough away from the scouts so that we can observe.

188
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:59,640
But we can't interfere without walking a bit of a distance.

189
00:15:00,140 --> 00:15:01,480
And camp gets set up.

190
00:15:01,540 --> 00:15:03,440
And the weekend goes like most of our meetings.

191
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:05,440
The senior patrol leader leads.

192
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:09,100
We observe and we participate when we're asked.

193
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:11,360
But otherwise, we're just watching.

194
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:14,540
Now, all of this sounds idyllic and wonderful, doesn't it?

195
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:19,080
It sounds like we just skip along without any problems or setbacks because we have these

196
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:21,040
hyper-competent uber scouts.

197
00:15:21,900 --> 00:15:24,240
And I got to tell you, nothing could be farther from the truth.

198
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:30,520
What I described to you was a very brief overview of an idealized kind of situation.

199
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:35,040
So when we're looking at the program with new eyes, one of the things, hopefully, that

200
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:38,079
we'll realize is that when the going is messy, things are working.

201
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,420
Our scouts are just like yours.

202
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:44,199
They're sometimes incompetent and cavalier and unmotivated.

203
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:47,480
They make mistakes and oversights, both large and small.

204
00:15:47,579 --> 00:15:49,760
They're subject to all the vicissitudes of their age.

205
00:15:50,439 --> 00:15:57,760
And we adults, me included, are often frustrated or disheartened and impatient and angry every

206
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:58,420
once in a while.

207
00:15:58,420 --> 00:16:00,480
But we don't want it any other way.

208
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:01,420
Not for a moment.

209
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:03,480
This is the way that it's supposed to be.

210
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:07,620
This is the process that builds character, leadership, and maturity.

211
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,420
It's messy, indefinite, and unpredictable.

212
00:16:11,079 --> 00:16:12,620
It is scouting.

213
00:16:12,819 --> 00:16:18,980
So you might ask, well, how in the world can you sit by and watch this go on?

214
00:16:19,100 --> 00:16:25,280
How can you let the boys go out there and, you know, maybe they've not planned properly

215
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:27,880
or something like that and they have a real rough time of it?

216
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:35,980
Well, we're always watching for anything that is going to cross the boundaries of propriety

217
00:16:35,980 --> 00:16:37,020
and safety.

218
00:16:37,020 --> 00:16:38,680
And we will step in.

219
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:46,520
